// @korulang/unikraft-mpi — Unikraft's mailbox (`ukmpi`'s `lib/ukmpi/mbox.c`), lifted.
//
//   import unikraft/mpi
//
// WHAT THIS IS. `lib/ukmpi` exports exactly 10 functions around one resource,
// `struct uk_mbox` — a fixed-capacity ring of `void *` slots guarded by a pair
// of counting semaphores (one token per free slot, one per filled slot). It is
// Unikraft's inter-thread mailbox: `post` blocks until a slot is free, `recv`
// blocks until a message arrives, and both come in blocking / `_try` /
// `_to` (timeout) flavours. `struct uk_mbox` itself is never defined in any
// public header — `include/uk/mbox.h` only forward-declares `struct uk_mbox;`
// — so unlike `blk`, `pages`, `sched` and `lock`, THIS LIFT NEEDS NO STRUCT
// MIRROR AT ALL. Nothing here reads a field of `struct uk_mbox`; every fact
// this module needs about a mailbox's contents is bookkeeping IT keeps itself,
// described below.
//
// THE ASSERT THAT NAMES THE CHALLENGE: `uk_mbox_free` (mbox.c:38) —
//
//     UK_ASSERT(m->readpos == m->writepos);
//
// — DRAIN BEFORE FREE. `readpos == writepos` means "every message ever posted
// has also been received": the ring is empty. With `CONFIG_LIBUKDEBUG_ENABLE_ASSERT`
// off, which is every image anyone ships, `uk_mbox_free` frees the backing
// allocation regardless, and any message still sitting in the ring — plus
// anyone still blocked in `uk_mbox_recv` waiting for one — is left pointing at
// freed memory. The C's own comment says the plain part out loud: "it is an
// indication of a programming error... and the developer should be notified."
// Assert-off, nobody is.
//
// THE RATCHET. Two states, not eight — see "Where pillar 4 does NOT apply"
// below for why this is deliberately smaller than `blk`'s or `vmem`'s.
//
//     create   mints  <open!>                                uk_mbox_create
//     post*    needs  <!open>  mints <open!>  (self-loop)     uk_mbox_post / _try / _to / _try_isr
//     recv*    needs  <!open>  mints <open!>  (self-loop)     uk_mbox_recv / _try / _to / _try_isr
//     drain    needs  <!open>  mints <drained!> ON EVIDENCE   NO C CALL — reads this lift's own count
//              or refuses, handing <open!> back with the count still outstanding
//     release  needs  <!drained>                              uk_mbox_free
//     abandon  needs  <!open>                                  NO C CALL — give up, leak the ring
//
// HOW `drain` LIFTS `mbox.c:38` WITHOUT A STRUCT MIRROR. `uk_mbox_free`
// exposes no way to ask a mailbox "are you empty" — there is no accessor for
// `readpos`/`writepos`, mirrored or otherwise. So this lift keeps its OWN
// count: every successful `post*` increments `pending`, every successful
// `recv*` decrements it, both via `@atomicRmw` so the count is safe to touch
// from more than one Koru caller even though `uk_mbox`'s ring itself already
// serializes through its semaphores. `drain` is the ONLY tor that can mint
// `<drained!>`, and it mints it ONLY when `pending == 0` — otherwise it hands
// the handle straight back in `<open!>`, still holding the obligation, with
// the outstanding count in the payload. `release` accepts nothing but
// `<drained!>`. So the program `uk_mbox_free` was written to catch —
//
//     create(4): m |> post(mbox: m, token: 1): m2 |> release(mbox: m2)
//
// — does not compile: `release` needs `<!drained>` and `m2` is `<open!>`.
// Inserting `drain(m2)` does not save it either: with one message posted and
// none received, `drain` refuses and hands `<open!>` back, so the only
// `release` reachable from there is one that runs after enough `recv*` calls
// bring `pending` back to zero. A caller who abandons the obligation instead
// — including by doing nothing and letting `abandon` auto-discharge at scope
// exit (see below) — never reaches `uk_mbox_free` either: `abandon` runs no
// C call at all. So `uk_mbox_free` provably never sees an undrained `m`
// through ANY path this module's surface admits, attended or not.
//
// THIS IS A DIFFERENT SHAPE OF LIFT THAN `blk`'S TRANSFER ASYMMETRY, and it
// is worth being precise about the difference rather than overclaiming the
// same kind of proof. `blk:stop` needs `<!used>`, and NOTHING mints `<used!>`
// except a real transfer — the unsafe call is unreachable by construction,
// zero runtime check, a pure type-level fact. `drain` is not that: whether
// `pending == 0` is a genuinely dynamic fact this lift cannot know until
// runtime, so `drain`'s mint is gated on a runtime read of a counter this
// module maintains, the same "mint on evidence" shape `sched` uses for
// `<ran!>` (the entry shim raises a flag; here, a successful `recv*` does).
// What IS proven at compile time, with no runtime component at all, is that
// `release` is unreachable from anywhere except a successful `drain` — the
// C's unconditional free becomes a free that can only ever run once this
// lift's own count says the ring is empty.
//
// A CORRECTION, earned by verifying rather than assuming: an earlier draft of
// this comment claimed a caller who ignores a `pending` refusal is "caught
// holding an obligation gate 3 will not let the program end with". That is
// imprecise. `abandon` below is void, takes only `mbox`, and is the UNIQUE
// such acceptor of `<!open>` — which makes it exactly the shape Koru's
// auto-discharge pass (`src/auto_discharge_inserter.zig`) splices in
// unattended at scope exit, silently, under default settings. A program that
// walks away from an open mailbox compiles clean; it does not leak-detect as
// a compile error the way a never-discharged `blk:Device` does (`blk:abandon`
// has the identical shape and is presumably auto-discharged the same way,
// unexercised there). Confirmed with `--auto-discharge=warn` (prints exactly
// which call it inserted) and `--auto-discharge=disable` (turns the same
// program back into a KORU030). This is NOT a compiler defect — auto-discharge
// is documented, intentional RAII-style cleanup, and "leak the ring, never
// corrupt it" is the same safe default this lift makes by hand everywhere
// else. The corrected, honest claim is the one two paragraphs up: `uk_mbox_free`
// never runs on an undrained ring, attended or not. Whether the KORU OBLIGATION
// itself is provably discharged on every path is a strictly weaker claim than
// this lift first made, and README.md's "A load-bearing finding" section
// carries the full evidence.
//
// THE SOUNDNESS BOUNDARY, stated rather than hidden. `pending`'s two writers
// (`post*`'s increment, `recv*`'s decrement) are not atomic WITH the C call
// that causes them — there is a window between "the message really landed in
// the ring" and "this lift's counter reflects it". A `drain` landing in that
// window against a CONCURRENTLY RUNNING `post` on another thread could read
// `pending == 0` and mint `<drained!>` a moment before a message actually
// lands. This matches the assumption the C's own comment makes — teardown
// happens once producers and consumers have quiesced, not while one is still
// live — and this lift does not claim more than the assert it replaces did.
// Freeing a mailbox while something might still be posting to it is not a
// safe program in the C either.
//
// WHERE PILLAR 4 DOES NOT APPLY, and why this reads differently than
// `unikraft/alloc`'s ruling on the same question. `alloc` IMPOSES an
// asymmetry the C does not have — `take(); free();` never touching what you
// allocated is legal C and is refused here anyway, because untouched memory
// freed unread is pointless by construction; there is no legitimate reason to
// allocate and never look at what you got. A mailbox does not have that
// property. `create(4); drain; release;` — a mailbox stood up and torn down
// having carried zero messages — is not pointless the way an unread
// allocation is: pre-wiring an event channel that a config path never ends up
// using, or unwinding cleanly on an error path taken before the first message
// would have gone out, are both real programs. The C agrees: `readpos ==
// writepos` at 0 == 0 satisfies the assert with room to spare. So this lift
// does NOT force a `post`/`recv` before `release` the way `blk` forces a
// transfer before `stop` — `drain` right after `create` is legal and cheap,
// and forcing ceremony there would be exactly the "feature-maximalism
// wearing a safety badge" the brief warns against.
//
// NO NAMED ESCAPE HATCH, unlike `blk:io.skipped` or `lock:hold.skipped`. Both
// of those exist because the gate they bypass costs something real (a device
// transfer, a lock acquisition) that a caller might legitimately not want to
// pay. `drain` costs a load of a Koru-owned integer — there is no honest
// program for which skipping that check buys anything, so this lift does not
// offer one.
//
// THE UNWRITTEN RULE THE ASSERT CENSUS DOES NOT COUNT: `uk_mbox_create` takes
// an allocator and `uk_mbox_free` takes one too, and nothing in the C checks
// they are the same one — passing `uk_mbox_free` a DIFFERENT `struct
// uk_alloc *` than the mailbox was built with hands its backing memory to an
// allocator that never owned it. No `UK_ASSERT` guards this at all; it is
// simply not exposed as a choice here. `create` reads `_uk_alloc_head` once
// and the `Mailbox` handle carries that same pointer to `release`, so a
// mismatched pairing is not a program this lift's surface can express.
//
// THE MESSAGE TYPE. `uk_mbox_post`/`recv` carry `void *msg` — the C never
// dereferences it, so it is opaque to `ukmpi` itself, not merely to this
// lift. Koru has no generics (this project's language rules: comptime
// codegen for containers, not a generics feature), so this lift carries it as
// `usize`, the pointer-width integer a `void *` bit-pattern already is. A
// caller who wants a real payload allocates it (`unikraft/alloc`, a shipped
// sibling, is the natural partner) and posts its address; `0` round-trips as
// a legitimate token, not as "no message" — this lift never treats it
// specially.
//
// ONE VALUE CHECK THAT DOES NOT BECOME A PHANTOM STATE. `uk_mbox_create`
// asserts `size <= __L_MAX` (mbox.c:10, `__L_MAX` = `LONG_MAX` = the C `long`
// max). Phantom states are symbolic, not value-dependent, so this is not
// lifted the way the ordering rule is — see the census below for how it is
// handled instead.

// ============================================================================
// UNIKRAFT C ABI
// ============================================================================
//
// Every symbol below is on `lib/ukmpi/exportsyms.uk` (10 lines, 10 links, 0
// phantom, 0 inert — the shelf's own measurement). `struct uk_mbox` has no
// public definition anywhere in the tree, so there is nothing here to mirror.

const UkMbox = opaque {};
const UkAlloc = opaque {};

// `uk_alloc_get_default()` is `static inline` and returns this exported global
// (lib/ukalloc/include/uk/alloc.h:136, lib/ukalloc/exportsyms.uk:22). Reading
// the variable is the same load the inline function performs. Same technique
// `blk`, `alloc`, `pages` and `sched` already use.
extern var _uk_alloc_head: ?*UkAlloc;

extern fn uk_mbox_create(a: *UkAlloc, size: usize) ?*UkMbox;
extern fn uk_mbox_free(a: *UkAlloc, m: *UkMbox) void;

extern fn uk_mbox_post(m: *UkMbox, msg: ?*anyopaque) void;
extern fn uk_mbox_post_try(m: *UkMbox, msg: ?*anyopaque) c_int;
extern fn uk_mbox_post_to(m: *UkMbox, msg: ?*anyopaque, timeout: u64) u64;
extern fn uk_mbox_post_try_isr(m: *UkMbox, msg: ?*anyopaque) c_int;

extern fn uk_mbox_recv(m: *UkMbox, msg: *?*anyopaque) void;
extern fn uk_mbox_recv_try(m: *UkMbox, msg: *?*anyopaque) c_int;
extern fn uk_mbox_recv_to(m: *UkMbox, msg: *?*anyopaque, timeout: u64) u64;
extern fn uk_mbox_recv_try_isr(m: *UkMbox, msg: *?*anyopaque) c_int;

// nolibc, backed by ukalloc's default allocator. Named `release`, not `free`,
// for the tor below so it never collides with this declaration.
extern fn malloc(size: usize) ?*anyopaque;
extern fn free(ptr: ?*anyopaque) void;

// __NSEC_MAX — include/uk/arch/time.h:54, `(__U64_MAX)`. What `_to` returns on
// a timeout; anything else is elapsed nanoseconds.
const NSEC_MAX: u64 = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;

comptime {
    // The retirement of `UK_ASSERT(size <= __L_MAX)` below depends on
    // `capacity` being a `u32` and `__L_MAX` being the 64-bit `LONG_MAX`
    // (include/uk/arch/limits.h:147, active on any 64-bit target — this lift
    // builds x86_64 only, per BUILD.md). If that ever stops holding, this
    // turns into a build error instead of a silently wrong claim.
    if (@bitSizeOf(usize) < 64) {
        @compileError("unikraft/mpi assumes a 64-bit size_t (__L_MAX = LONG_MAX on x86_64); not checked on a narrower target");
    }
}

// ============================================================================
// THE HANDLE
// ============================================================================
//
// Koru-owned, not `extern`: it never crosses into C except through `mbox` and
// `alloc`, both opaque pointers the C already gave out. `pending` is this
// lift's entire answer to "is the ring empty" — see the header note.

const Mailbox = struct {
    mbox: *UkMbox,
    alloc: *UkAlloc,
    pending: i64,
};

fn tokenToPtr(token: usize) ?*anyopaque {
    if (token == 0) return null;
    return @ptrFromInt(token);
}

fn ptrToToken(ptr: ?*anyopaque) usize {
    if (ptr) |p| return @intFromPtr(p);
    return 0;
}

fn destroy(mb: *Mailbox) void {
    free(@ptrCast(mb));
}

// ============================================================================
// CREATE
// ============================================================================

// uk_mbox_create. `capacity` is the ring's slot count — `size` in the C,
// which allocates `size + 1` slots internally (mbox.c:16) so the semaphore
// pair can distinguish full from empty; that off-by-one is the C's own
// bookkeeping and this lift does not re-expose it as a caller-visible detail.
//
// `size <= __L_MAX` (mbox.c:10) is a VALUE check, not an ordering one — see
// the comptime block above for the only sense in which this lift retires it.
// Not lifted as a phantom state on principle: the brief's own rule is that
// symbolic states cannot encode a value comparison, and `blk`'s `nb_queues`
// bound is the precedent for treating this class as a runtime `refused`/
// `failed` arm instead. Here it never even reaches that arm on this lift's
// build target, because `u32::max < __L_MAX` on 64-bit — asserted, not
// assumed, by the comptime block above.
//
// Mints <open!>. No named escape from `create` failing: no allocator, no
// backing memory, nothing to hand back but a reason.
~pub tor create { capacity: u32 }
| ok *Mailbox<open!>
| failed string

~proc create|zig {
    const a = _uk_alloc_head orelse {
        return .{ .failed = "no default allocator registered (_uk_alloc_head is null)" };
    };
    const raw = uk_mbox_create(a, @as(usize, capacity)) orelse {
        return .{ .failed = "uk_mbox_create returned NULL (out of memory)" };
    };
    const mem = malloc(@sizeOf(Mailbox)) orelse {
        uk_mbox_free(a, raw);
        return .{ .failed = "out of memory allocating the handle wrapper" };
    };
    const mb: *Mailbox = @ptrCast(@alignCast(mem));
    mb.* = Mailbox{ .mbox = raw, .alloc = a, .pending = 0 };
    return .{ .ok = mb };
}

// ============================================================================
// POST — blocking / try / timeout / isr-safe try
// ============================================================================
//
// All four are the SAME self-loop on <!open> -> <open!>: posting changes
// nothing about what a caller may do next. `mbox.c` guards each with only
// `UK_ASSERT(m)` (null-pointer hygiene — see the census), so there is no
// ordering rule left over here for the phantom state to carry; the branch
// payload is exactly "what happened", per the brief's own rule for this
// shape.

// uk_mbox_post. Blocks until a slot is free. Cannot fail — a caller who wants
// a non-blocking or bounded-wait attempt reaches for `post.try`/`post.timeout`
// instead, which is the same choice the C's own three-function family offers.
~pub tor post { mbox: *Mailbox<!open>, token: usize } -> *Mailbox<open!>

~proc post|zig {
    uk_mbox_post(mbox.mbox, tokenToPtr(token));
    _ = @atomicRmw(i64, &mbox.pending, .Add, 1, .monotonic);
    return mbox;
}

// uk_mbox_post_try. Non-blocking: `full` when every slot is taken
// (uk_semaphore_down_try on the write semaphore fails, C returns -ENOBUFS).
~pub tor post.try { mbox: *Mailbox<!open>, token: usize }
| ok *Mailbox<open!>
| full *Mailbox<open!>

~proc post.try|zig {
    const rc = uk_mbox_post_try(mbox.mbox, tokenToPtr(token));
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .full = mbox };
    }
    _ = @atomicRmw(i64, &mbox.pending, .Add, 1, .monotonic);
    return .{ .ok = mbox };
}

// uk_mbox_post_to. Blocks at most `timeout_ns`. `waited_ns` is the elapsed
// time the C itself measured (uk_semaphore_down_to's return value); `timeout`
// is `uk_mbox_post_to` returning `__NSEC_MAX`.
~pub tor post.timeout { mbox: *Mailbox<!open>, token: usize, timeout_ns: u64 }
| ok { mbox: *Mailbox<open!>, waited_ns: u64 }
| timeout *Mailbox<open!>

~proc post.timeout|zig {
    const ret = uk_mbox_post_to(mbox.mbox, tokenToPtr(token), timeout_ns);
    if (ret == NSEC_MAX) {
        return .{ .timeout = mbox };
    }
    _ = @atomicRmw(i64, &mbox.pending, .Add, 1, .monotonic);
    return .{ .ok = .{ .mbox = mbox, .waited_ns = ret } };
}

// uk_mbox_post_try_isr. Same non-blocking attempt as `post.try`, over
// `uk_semaphore_down_try_isr` instead of `uk_semaphore_down_try` — safe to
// call from interrupt context because it never touches the scheduler. This
// lift does not set up a Koru-callable interrupt handler, so the boot demo
// exercises this from ordinary thread context, where it behaves identically;
// see README.md for what that does and does not prove.
~pub tor post.try-isr { mbox: *Mailbox<!open>, token: usize }
| ok *Mailbox<open!>
| full *Mailbox<open!>

~proc post.try-isr|zig {
    const rc = uk_mbox_post_try_isr(mbox.mbox, tokenToPtr(token));
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .full = mbox };
    }
    _ = @atomicRmw(i64, &mbox.pending, .Add, 1, .monotonic);
    return .{ .ok = mbox };
}

// ============================================================================
// RECV — blocking / try / timeout / isr-safe try
// ============================================================================

// uk_mbox_recv. Blocks until a message arrives. Cannot fail.
~pub tor recv { mbox: *Mailbox<!open> } -> { mbox: *Mailbox<open!>, token: usize }

~proc recv|zig {
    var raw: ?*anyopaque = null;
    uk_mbox_recv(mbox.mbox, &raw);
    _ = @atomicRmw(i64, &mbox.pending, .Sub, 1, .monotonic);
    return .{ .mbox = mbox, .token = ptrToToken(raw) };
}

// uk_mbox_recv_try. Non-blocking: `empty` when nothing is queued
// (C returns -ENOMSG).
~pub tor recv.try { mbox: *Mailbox<!open> }
| ok { mbox: *Mailbox<open!>, token: usize }
| empty *Mailbox<open!>

~proc recv.try|zig {
    var raw: ?*anyopaque = null;
    const rc = uk_mbox_recv_try(mbox.mbox, &raw);
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .empty = mbox };
    }
    _ = @atomicRmw(i64, &mbox.pending, .Sub, 1, .monotonic);
    return .{ .ok = .{ .mbox = mbox, .token = ptrToToken(raw) } };
}

// uk_mbox_recv_to. Blocks at most `timeout_ns`.
~pub tor recv.timeout { mbox: *Mailbox<!open>, timeout_ns: u64 }
| ok { mbox: *Mailbox<open!>, token: usize, waited_ns: u64 }
| timeout *Mailbox<open!>

~proc recv.timeout|zig {
    var raw: ?*anyopaque = null;
    const ret = uk_mbox_recv_to(mbox.mbox, &raw, timeout_ns);
    if (ret == NSEC_MAX) {
        return .{ .timeout = mbox };
    }
    _ = @atomicRmw(i64, &mbox.pending, .Sub, 1, .monotonic);
    return .{ .ok = .{ .mbox = mbox, .token = ptrToToken(raw), .waited_ns = ret } };
}

// uk_mbox_recv_try_isr. Same shape as `post.try-isr` — see that tor's note.
~pub tor recv.try-isr { mbox: *Mailbox<!open> }
| ok { mbox: *Mailbox<open!>, token: usize }
| empty *Mailbox<open!>

~proc recv.try-isr|zig {
    var raw: ?*anyopaque = null;
    const rc = uk_mbox_recv_try_isr(mbox.mbox, &raw);
    if (rc != 0) {
        return .{ .empty = mbox };
    }
    _ = @atomicRmw(i64, &mbox.pending, .Sub, 1, .monotonic);
    return .{ .ok = .{ .mbox = mbox, .token = ptrToToken(raw) } };
}

// ============================================================================
// INTROSPECTION — no obligation, same rule blk's `count` and vmem's `lookup` use
// ============================================================================

// Reads this lift's own counter. No C call, no obligation: knowing how many
// messages are outstanding constrains nothing about what a caller may do
// next — `post*`/`recv*` remain legal either way, and so does attempting
// `drain` (which will simply refuse again if the count is not yet zero).
~pub tor pending { mbox: *Mailbox<!open> } -> { mbox: *Mailbox<open!>, count: i64 }

~proc pending|zig {
    const p = @atomicLoad(i64, &mbox.pending, .monotonic);
    return .{ .mbox = mbox, .count = p };
}

// ============================================================================
// DRAIN — the ONLY way to mint <drained!>, on evidence
// ============================================================================
//
// Lifts mbox.c:38 (`uk_mbox_free`: `UK_ASSERT(m->readpos == m->writepos)`).
// See the header note for the exact shape of this retirement and its
// soundness boundary. `pending != 0` refuses and hands the SAME obligation
// straight back — this is a retry point, not a dead end; there is no
// `abandon` because there is nothing to abandon into. Keep calling `recv*`
// and try again.
~pub tor drain { mbox: *Mailbox<!open> }
| ok *Mailbox<drained!>
| pending { mbox: *Mailbox<open!>, count: i64 }

~proc drain|zig {
    const p = @atomicLoad(i64, &mbox.pending, .monotonic);
    if (p != 0) {
        return .{ .pending = .{ .mbox = mbox, .count = p } };
    }
    return .{ .ok = mbox };
}

// ============================================================================
// RELEASE
// ============================================================================
//
// uk_mbox_free. Named `release`, matching `blk`, not `free` — this file
// already declares the C `free()` used by `destroy` above, and this tor's
// job is that C call plus this lift's own allocation, so keeping the two
// spellings apart is not optional here the way it was cosmetic elsewhere.
//
// Takes ONLY <!drained>. `uk_mbox_free` is `void` — it cannot fail — so
// there is no failure arm to design around; the interesting refusal already
// happened at `drain`.
~pub tor release { mbox: *Mailbox<!drained> }

~proc release|zig {
    uk_mbox_free(mbox.alloc, mbox.mbox);
    destroy(mbox);
}

// The one way out of <open!> that does not require draining first: give up
// on the mailbox rather than either corrupt it (calling `uk_mbox_free`
// directly on an undrained ring would be exactly the bug mbox.c:38 exists to
// catch) or hang forever retrying `drain`. Frees only this lift's own handle
// wrapper; the C-side ring and whatever it still holds are deliberately
// leaked, never freed. Named and greppable, matching `blk:abandon`: "we gave
// up on a mailbox" should be a search, not an inference from a missing
// `release` call.
~pub tor abandon { mbox: *Mailbox<!open> }

~proc abandon|zig {
    destroy(mbox);
}
